response:
When choosing between the pre-built LMS bundle and hiring a vendor for custom onboarding workshops, I’d focus on how well each approach aligns with your program goals, the training quality, and ongoing support.&#x20;



If the goal is to deliver a more polished and memorable onboarding experience, custom workshops may be worth it once: the vendor can craft content that feels specific to your org, involve higher-quality content curation and facilitation, and help drive stronger engagement. This approach also means less setup and more hands-off management afterward.



For a quarter-long rollout, the total cost—fees, staff time, and impact—definitely matters, so it’s reasonable to estimate total spend for both paths. Since the LMS bundle covers more of the initial setup, its higher upfront cost may be justified by the faster launch pace, whereas the custom route could be framed as an investment in lasting quality and higher participant satisfaction. Comparing the associated pros and trade-offs is essential.
